3. CORE ISOLATION MATRIX: LINUX KERNEL MECHANISMS

Once the deployment agent initializes, the system relies on five low-level Linux kernel primitives to lock down the Memory Custody boundaries against local manipulation, dumpers, or inspection by automated AI exploitation tools.
• Page Table Stripping (_PAGE_NX): Modifies bit flags in the architectural CPU page tables via memory management (mm) kernel functions. The driver switches page access flags to a strict No-Execute state and drops user-space pointer readability. Local read queries trigger an immediate hardware exception (SIGSEGV), blocking local software while allowing the network DMA stack to read/write raw memory lines.
• Hardware PKEY Allocation: Ties the active custody DRAM pages to a target Protection Key allocation (pkey_alloc). The processor physically bars access to these registers even if a local process manages to execute a kernel-space exploit, unless the active execution thread possesses the specific signature key running inside the CPU’s PKRU register.
• MemBlock Subsumption: Bypasses standard logical memory zones (ZONE_NORMAL, ZONE_DMA32) entirely during initial kernel boot mapping. By carving out the hardware early, the isolated 10GB array physically ceases to exist for the guest OS, rendering diagnostic tools like /dev/mem or indexing scans unable to find or reference the target memory range.
• XDP eBPF Data Ingestion: Bypasses standard OS network layers by dropping an eBPF (Extended Berkeley Packet Filter) program directly onto the network interface controller using the XDP (eXpress Data Path) layer. Encrypted network data blocks from the cloud are caught and written to the DRAM vault immediately via DMA, dropping the original source packets before they hit standard kernel diagnostic tools to mitigate local sniffing loops (e.g., tcpdump).
• Active Cache Line Purging: Evaporates transient computing residues immediately after processing vertical buffer payloads. The driver calls machine-level assembly instructions CLFLUSH coupled with MFENCE serialization blocks, ensuring processed mathematical states are wiped cleanly from CPU L1/L2/L3 cache blocks.

